course: dictionary
lesson: 8520
title: Naming kitchen things
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A noun is a word for a person, place or thing. A saucepan is a noun, and it names a thing you find in most kitchens. Its meaning is a deep, round metal pot with a long handle and often a lid, used on top of a cooker for boiling and heating food.
explain: Context tells you what a saucepan is for. In «A toddler in Ohio pulled on a saucepan handle sticking out over the edge of the cooker», the handle sticks out from something hot on a cooker, so a saucepan is a cooking pot. Check it by swapping in cooking pot: the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Stories can use a word in surprising ways. In Alice's story, the cook throws «saucepans, plates and dishes», which shows they are kitchen things. Then the Mouse's words, «like a pea in a saucepan», compare a hopping creature to something bouncing around inside a pan, so a saucepan works like a container. Look for the clues around the word.
fact: A saucepan is a deep, round metal cooking pot with a long handle.
fact: Many saucepans have a lid, which keeps heat and steam in.
fact: Saucepans sit on a cooker and are used to boil eggs, cook pasta or warm milk.
fact: «Turn the handle in» means turning it so it does not stick out over the edge of the cooker.
fact: A toddler who pulls a hot saucepan handle can get serious burns, which is why handles must be turned inward.
exercise: «The cook throws fire-irons, saucepans, plates and dishes at the Duchess.» What is a saucepan? | A small bird that sings | *A deep, round metal pot used for cooking | A fast horse with a long tail | A soft pillow for sleeping
exercise: Mia wants to warm some milk. Which kitchen thing should she use? | A pair of gloves | A bath towel | A garden hose | *A saucepan on the cooker
exercise: Find and copy the noun that names a kitchen pot in this sentence: «The toddler pulled on a saucepan handle.» | handle | *saucepan | toddler | pulled
test: Why should you turn a saucepan handle inward on a cooker? | So the pot can fly | So the lid stays on | So it looks neat in the kitchen | *So nobody knocks it over and gets burned
test: «He hopped like a pea in a saucepan.» What does this suggest about the saucepan? | It is a garden tool | It is a sleeping place | *It is a container that things can bounce around inside | It is always full of pasta
exam: Which sentence uses the word saucepan correctly? | *Saucepan is a noun, so I can put the saucepan on the cooker to boil water, and it names a thing | Saucepan means a fast running sport | Saucepan is a noun, so I can put the saucepan on the cooker to boil water | The saucepan is a feeling of being sad
